cff-version: 1.2.0
title: "Self-undermining complexity: five experiments on complexification and criticality"
message: "If you use this software or its results, please cite it."
type: software
authors:
  - family-names: "Rodriguez Suarez"
    given-names: "Daniel"
    email: "dan@ultranarrative.com"
repository-code: "https://github.com/ultranarrative/self-undermining-complexity"
abstract: >-
  Five computational experiments testing whether systems that grow their own
  complexity drive themselves toward their own instability. Across three
  substrates sharing no mechanism, an ecological assembly model, a Hopfield
  associative memory and a random Boolean network, accretion reaches the
  substrate's critical point when new elements degrade existing ones and not
  otherwise. Includes reproductions of Grilli, Rogers and Allesina (2016) and of
  Biroli, Bunin and Cammarota (2018), both credited as reproductions, together
  with the negative results and the corrections that overturned two earlier
  claims.
keywords:
  - complexity
  - self-organised criticality
  - stability
  - Lotka-Volterra
  - random matrix theory
  - Hopfield networks
  - early-warning signals
license: MIT
